>What good is money besides buying stuff and raising your score?  How
>does it raise your score, do you have to have it on you when the game
>ends?

Yes, you have to have the money on you.

>What good are gems besides selling in shops and giving unicorns?

Also, when you die or win it raises your score a LOT.  Diamonds are not
the only valuable ones, either.  But MOST gems that you get early on are
worthless pieces of <insert your favorite color here> glass.

>Are unicorns good for anything besides raising your luck?

Geez, isn't that good enough?  Giving an unidentified gem to a unicorn
gives you +1, and giving her an identified VALUABLE one (a jade stone,
etc.) gives you +5.  Having luck of 5 or more just about guarentees you'll
never miss when you swing at a monster.

>How do you get close enough to a unicorn to give it a gem?  I find them
>to be much too elusive.

Patience.  You have to be in a direct firing line with her and throw the
gem at her.  (You miss the unicorn..-more-  The unicorn graciously accepts
your gift.)  Basically, you just have to chase her around and hope you
catch her off guard.  Or, try to corner her into a trap.  If you have a pick-
axe, you can make one yourself.

>Why do wands of wishing not always work ("unfortunately, nothing happens")?
>Does this have something to do with your luck?

Yes.
The code generates a random number (either 0 or 1) when you zap a wand
of wishing.  It then adds this number to your current luck.  If it's positive,
it gives you the wish.  If it's 0 or negative, you get "Unfortunately,
nothing happens." Therefore, if you have negative luck, you can't get ever
get a wish.

>What does the ring of stealth do?

Ya know how when you walk into a killer bee hive or David's Treasure
Zoo, all the monsters head right for you?  If you have a ring of
stealth on, they don't see you, and you can walk in pretty much unnoticed.
This is handy in big zoos, because then you can walk in and bash on
monsters one at a time without being surrounded and getting beat on
from all sides.

>What does the wand of cancellation do?

If you zap a L, he can't rob you.  N, she can't steal from you.
E, it won't freeze you.  R, it can't rust your armor.  Et cetera.

Also, I think your list of effects when you eat dead monsters is
not quite right in a lot of places.  In general, things are edible
at first, and then they go rotten (obviously, there are exceptions).

